# modules/iac/kubernetes.rules - Kubernetes manifest checks for the
# pattern-rule engine (docs/DESIGN.md §13 step 4, §6.6's Kubernetes bullet;
# this ticket).
#
# Path note: same frozen-path reasoning as modules/iac/terraform.rules'
# own header - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table grants the pattern-rule
# schema to `modules/iac/*.rules` FLAT.  This file is `kubernetes.rules`,
# never `checks.rules` (reserved repo-wide for the §9.5 script-check schema
# and matched first in records_schema_for_path) and never nested under a
# `rules/` subdirectory the way modules/sast/rules/*.rules is.
#
# Scope discipline (this ticket's own text): plain Kubernetes YAML/JSON
# manifests ONLY.  Docker/Dockerfile, docker-compose, Helm chart TEMPLATES,
# and CloudFormation are explicitly out of scope - §6.6 groups all of those
# under one `containers.rules` name, but terraform.rules already established
# the precedent of one flat file per technology rather than the grouped
# name, and this ticket's acceptance criteria name `modules/iac/kubernetes.rules`
# specifically.  Docker/compose/Helm-template scanning is future work for a
# separate ticket; this file's only obligation toward Helm is the negative
# one - stay silent on a Helm-template-shaped file.
#
# `files` intentionally covers *.yaml, *.yml, AND *.json: a Kubernetes
# manifest is valid in any of the three, and CloudFormation templates and
# Helm-rendered charts use the exact same extensions - the glob cannot be
# the thing that tells them apart (this ticket's own acceptance criteria say
# so explicitly for CloudFormation).  Two different content-based mechanisms
# do that job instead:
#
# 1. CloudFormation exclusion is primarily a STRUCTURAL property of the
#    patterns themselves, not a context-deny.  Every Kubernetes API field
#    this pack keys on - `privileged`, `hostNetwork`, `hostPID`, `containers`,
#    `image`, `resources`, `runAsNonRoot`, `imagePullPolicy`,
#    `automountServiceAccountToken`, `kind`, `verbs` - is lowerCamelCase in
#    the Kubernetes API, and matching (§8.2) is case-sensitive.
#    CloudFormation's equivalent concepts (ECS `ContainerDefinitions`,
#    `Privileged`, `Image`, IAM `Action`/`Resource`) are PascalCase by AWS-wide
#    convention, and CFN has no `kind:` field at all (its own top-level keys
#    are `AWSTemplateFormatVersion`/`Resources`/`Type`).  A pattern anchored on
#    the exact lowercase spelling therefore never matches CloudFormation
#    regardless of file extension.  The three ABSENCE checks
#    (IAC-K8S-MISSING_RESOURCE_LIMITS-01, IAC-K8S-RUN_AS_ROOT-01,
#    IAC-K8S-SA_TOKEN_DEFAULT-01) additionally carry an
#    `AWSTemplateFormatVersion|AWS::` context-deny as belt-and-suspenders,
#    since an absence check fires on the ABSENCE of a nearby token and a
#    second, independent signal costs nothing once the record already has a
#    context-window open for the Helm guard below.
#
# 2. Helm chart TEMPLATE exclusion is a `\{\{` context-deny on the three
#    absence checks above.  A Helm template replaces literal risk values
#    with `{{ .Values.x }}` expressions, so the five PRESENCE checks
#    (privileged, host-namespace, secret-env, mutable-tag, RBAC-wildcard)
#    are already immune - there is no literal `true`/`:latest`/bare `"*"` for
#    them to match once the value is templated.  The three ABSENCE checks
#    are the genuine risk: they fire when a literal guard token is missing
#    nearby, and a Helm template's guard token is ALSO missing (it is
#    templated, not literal), which would otherwise be a false positive on
#    every Helm-rendered chart.  The `\{\{` deny suppresses exactly that
#    case, on the same window already open for the guard token itself.
#
# Both mechanisms are line-oriented and window-bounded (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md
# §10, context-window capped at 50 by E032), consistent with the rest of
# this pattern-rule engine - this is not a YAML/JSON parser, so a minified
# single-line JSON document (the entire manifest as one line) is a known,
# documented gap rather than a silent one, exactly like the KMS
# key-rotation absence check in terraform.rules already accepts a bounded
# window over a hypothetical arbitrarily-long resource block.  Field names
# are matched with an optional surrounding quote (`"?name"?`) so the same
# pattern covers both YAML's usually-unquoted keys and JSON's always-quoted
# ones.
#
# Each absence check's W033 warning (context-deny with context-window above
# 0) is the same accepted, reviewed trade-off IAC-TF-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01
# already carries: the window must be wide enough to reach the rest of a
# short manifest, and docs/FOUNDATION.md finding F4 (§10.2) already covers
# the reasoning for taking that trade knowingly rather than avoiding the
# warning by narrowing the window until the check no longer works.

id: IAC-K8S-PRIVILEGED-01
title: Container runs with privileged escalation enabled
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A05:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?privileged"?\s*:\s*"?true"?\b
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Remove `privileged: true` from the container's securityContext.
  A privileged container has near-complete access to the host - device nodes, kernel
  capabilities, and namespaces - so almost every real workload can run instead with a
  narrow, explicit `capabilities.add` list for only the capability it actually needs.
references: CWE-269

id: IAC-K8S-HOST_NAMESPACE-01
title: Pod shares the host network or PID namespace
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A05:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?(hostNetwork|hostPID)"?\s*:\s*"?true"?\b
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Set hostNetwork and hostPID to false (or omit them; both default to false).
  A pod in the host's network namespace can sniff and spoof traffic for every other pod on
  the node, and one in the host PID namespace can see and signal every process on the
  node, including other tenants' workloads.
references: CWE-284

id: IAC-K8S-MISSING_RESOURCE_LIMITS-01
title: Container has no resource limits or requests
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-400
owasp: A05:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?image"?\s*:\s*\S+
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
context-deny: ^\s*"?resources"?\s*:
context-deny: \{\{
context-deny: AWSTemplateFormatVersion|AWS::
context-window: 20
tags: static
remediation: Add a resources block with both limits and requests for cpu and memory.
  A container with no ceiling can starve every other workload on the node (or be starved
  by one), and the scheduler cannot bin-pack a pod whose requests are unset.
references: CWE-400

id: IAC-K8S-RUN_AS_ROOT-01
title: Container has no explicit non-root execution guard
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A05:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?containers"?\s*:
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
context-deny: "?runAsNonRoot"?\s*:\s*"?true"?\b
context-deny: \{\{
context-deny: AWSTemplateFormatVersion|AWS::
context-window: 20
tags: static
remediation: Set securityContext.runAsNonRoot to true (and pair it with a non-zero
  runAsUser) at the pod or container level. Without it, the container image's own
  default user applies, which for most public base images is root - a container
  breakout then lands the attacker in the host's root context rather than an
  unprivileged one.
references: CWE-269

id: IAC-K8S-SECRET_ENV-01
title: Plaintext credential-shaped literal in a container environment variable
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?value"?\s*:\s*["'][^"'[:space:]]{4,}["']\s*,?\s*$
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
context-require: "?name"?\s*:\s*"?[A-Za-z0-9_]*(PASSWORD|PASSWD|SECRET|TOKEN|API_KEY|CREDENTIAL)[A-Za-z0-9_]*"?
context-deny: (CHANGEME|changeme|EXAMPLE|example|XXXXXXXX|xxxxxxxx|placeholder|dummy)
context-deny: \{\{
context-window: 1
tags: static
remediation: Remove the literal value and source it via valueFrom.secretKeyRef (backed by
  a Kubernetes Secret, ideally itself populated from Vault/AWS Secrets Manager/SSM rather
  than committed) instead of a literal `value:` in the manifest. A value literal in a
  manifest lands in git history and in `kubectl get -o yaml` for anyone with read access
  to the resource.
references: CWE-798

id: IAC-K8S-MUTABLE_TAG-01
title: Container image pinned to the mutable :latest tag
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-494
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?image"?\s*:\s*"?\S*:latest\b
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
context-deny: \{\{
context-window: 0
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Pin the image to an immutable reference - a specific semver tag the registry
  will not overwrite, or better, a content-addressed digest (`@sha256:...`). `:latest`
  (or no tag at all, which defaults to it) means the same manifest can deploy a different
  image tomorrow with no corresponding change to review, and a rollback cannot pin to
  "what was actually running."
references: CWE-494

id: IAC-K8S-RBAC_WILDCARD-01
title: RBAC rule grants a wildcard verb or resource
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
pattern: ^\s*-?\s*['"]\*['"]\s*,?\s*$
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
context-require: "?(verbs|resources)"?\s*:
context-window: 3
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Enumerate the specific verbs (get, list, watch, ...) and resource types the
  role actually needs instead of `"*"`. A wildcarded Role or ClusterRole grants every
  future API type and every future verb Kubernetes ever adds, not just the ones that
  exist today, so the blast radius of a compromised service account grows with every
  Kubernetes upgrade.
references: CWE-269

id: IAC-K8S-SA_TOKEN_DEFAULT-01
title: Pod does not disable automatic service-account token mounting
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-269
owasp: A01:2021
pattern: ^\s*"?kind"?\s*:\s*"?(Pod|Deployment|StatefulSet|DaemonSet|ReplicaSet|Job|CronJob)"?\b
dialect: ere
files: *.yaml
files: *.yml
files: *.json
context-deny: "?automountServiceAccountToken"?\s*:\s*"?false"?\b
context-deny: \{\{
context-deny: AWSTemplateFormatVersion|AWS::
context-window: 20
tags: static
remediation: Set automountServiceAccountToken to false on the pod spec (or the
  ServiceAccount) unless the workload genuinely calls the Kubernetes API. The default is
  true, so every pod otherwise carries a live, mountable credential for its service
  account into a container that most workloads never use it from - exactly what a
  container-escape or SSRF exploit looks for next.
references: CWE-269
